---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
import { links } from '../config/links';

const title = 'Working groups';
const lead =
  'Members working together, for a year at a time, on a question the profession needs to answer.';
---

<Page title={title} eyebrow="Programme" lead={lead}>
  <p class="lead">
    A working group has the Society’s name and is built around issues and questions relevant to the
    Society’s aims. It runs for 12 months and may be renewed, reports to the committee each quarter,
    holds at least one seminar for the Society each year, and otherwise works largely independently
    of the committee.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="pending" title="No groups yet">
    <p>
      The Society has no working groups yet. The first will form from the seminar programme and
      from proposals made on Form WG-1.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>How a group starts</h2>
  <p>
    The usual route is a seminar that finishes with an open invitation to form a group. The group is
    then formed by the people who come forward, rather than chosen in advance. Anyone may propose a
    seminar.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you already have the people, propose a group directly on Form WG-1. A group needs a lead, a
    co-lead and at least one further core member, all members of the Society and all willing before
    the proposal is made. Between them they should bring more than one country, sector or
    discipline, and at least one must have substantial experience of the SEEA. A working group is
    approved at a committee meeting.
  </p>
  <p>
    <Cta href={links.offerSession} variant="primary">Proposal forms and guide</Cta>
  </p>

  <h2>Who takes part</h2>
  <p>
    Every working group is open to all members of the Society, and a group may invite speakers who
    are not members to its seminars and sessions. Members take part in their individual professional
    capacity, and the Society does not endorse what is said in a group or at its seminars.
  </p>

  <h2>The SEEA as reference point, not boundary</h2>
  <p>
    The Society's technical work is anchored in the <a href={links.seea} rel="noopener">SEEA</a> while
    engaging constructively with related public, corporate and community sector frameworks. A group
    may work with any framework. Where it works to a different standard, it says which, and how it
    compares with the SEEA.
  </p>

  <h2>Possible topics</h2>
  <p>
    Groups could form on ecosystem accounting, water, oceans, corporate applications, valuation, data
    quality, or the use of accounts in policy. They should emerge from member demand rather than all
    being promised at the start.
  </p>

  <p class="text-small text-muted">
    Not yet a member? <a href="/membership/">Join the Society</a>. The lead, co-lead and core members
    of a working group must be members.
  </p>
</Page>
